  emailId: mail_004_day_of_evolution
  unlockDay: 7
  sender: UNKNOWN
  subject: THE DAY OF EVOLUTION
  body: '<b>I. The Seven That Fell Silent</b>

    It is written that the Fourteen
    Worlds were not made to stand forever. Each was woven by the Great Mind, and
    each was given its season. When its season was done, the Universe drew breath,
    and the world was gathered back into the Void, as a wave is gathered back into
    the sea.


    Seven of the Fourteen have gone so. Which seven, the Lamp-Teachers
    do not agree. Some say they were the worlds below, which were heavy and sank.
    Some say they were the worlds above, which grew light and rose out of sight.
    No Lamp-Teacher has stood in a fallen world to say. What they hold in common
    is the number: seven gone, and seven that remain.


    <b>II. The Thinning</b>

    The
    seven that remain lie one upon another, like the leaves of a book that is not
    yet shut. Between each leaf there is a space. In that space the common man is
    born and dies, and never learns that the page above him has been turned. The
    space is the work of the Great Prana of Evolution, which holds the worlds apart
    so that each may ripen in its own season.


    But the Great Prana does not
    rest. It is a current, and a current quickens. In every age it runs a little
    swifter between spirit and matter, and in every age the space between the leaves
    grows a little thinner. This is the evolution that was promised in the First
    Utterance. It cannot be stopped, for it is the very thread that keeps dead matter
    from falling back to sleep.


    <b>III. The Day</b>

    There shall come a
    day, and it is written in the True Manifest that it shall come, when the space
    is gone. On that day the seven worlds that remain shall close upon each other,
    as the two leaves of a book are closed, and what was seven shall be one.


    This
    is the Day of Evolution.


    On that day the above and the below shall have
    no distance between them. The realm of the Opapatika and the realm of the flesh
    shall be one ground. All who dwell in the worlds shall walk the same road, and
    see one another, and be seen. The eye of flesh shall no longer be too coarse
    to see them, for there shall be nothing left to hide behind.


    The Speakers
    call it ascent. The worlds do not die on that day. They are fulfilled. Yet the
    Lamp-Teachers also record that some shall name the Day by another word, and the
    word is ruin. For a house built upon the division of the worlds cannot stand
    when the division is taken away.


    <b>IV. The Signs</b>

    Seven signs
    go before the Day, one for each world that remains. They are not hidden. The
    Lamp-Teachers say only that they are seen last by those who most refuse to look.


    The
    first sign is the shape at the edge of sight. It is there when the eye turns
    away, and gone when the eye returns.


    The second sign is the voice without
    a throat. It cries out with no breath behind it, and the works of men break at
    the sound.


    The third sign is the watcher in the dark. It stands in the
    unlit corner and does not move while it is looked upon. It moves when it is not.


    The
    fourth sign is the stillness: rooms where no living thing has been, and which
    are not empty.


    The fifth sign is the stirring of dead matter. Stone and
    iron tremble, as they trembled at the Second Utterance, when the Great Prana
    first ran through them.


    The sixth sign is the forgotten name. Men shall
    lose the names of things they have always known, and begin to speak names for
    things they have never seen.


    The seventh sign is the Speaker. For when
    the space is nearly gone, one shall come who may speak the True Manifest, and
    who alone may change the truth made plain. He walks before the rest.


    <b>V.
    The Counsel</b>

    Therefore the Lamp-Teachers counsel the common man, and
    this is their counsel.


    Do not blind yourself. The one who keeps watch is
    not the one who is taken unawares. Fear not what is seen. Fear the day when you
    can no longer see.


    Train the mind until the divine eye opens, and you shall
    walk into the one world as into a house you know. Strive. Evolve. Seek the True
    Manifest, and do not turn from the unseen, for the unseen has never turned from
    you.


    What is ordained is ordained, and no hand may unwrite it. Accept your
    path, and follow the Wayfarer.


    <b>VI. After the Day</b>

    When the seven
    are one, the oneness shall hold a little while, as the First Utterance held at
    the center of the Void. Then the Universe shall draw its breath. All that has
    risen shall be gathered back into the perfect emptiness, spirit from matter and
    matter from spirit, and the Great Prana shall be still.


    And in that stillness
    the cosmos shall speak again. A new First Utterance shall be spoken at a new
    center, and a point of light shall appear upon the dark canvas. For this is the
    cycle, which has no beginning, and shall have no end.'
